{# ONE BADGE: `[dot] [icon] label [suffix]`, tinted by `tone`.

   FOUR COMPONENTS FOLDED IN HERE, and each was a badge with one detail added:
   the pill was a badge with a dot, the claim was a badge with a lock glyph and a
   machine id, the refresh indicator was a badge with a relative time after the
   label, and the dot was that detail on its own — no props, no story reference,
   four composers. Every one of the four carried its own copy of the tint table
   below, which is the whole reason there is now one.

   THE DOT IS THE DELETED COMPONENT'S MARKUP, INLINED — one span, one colour from
   the map, `aria-hidden`. It stays `aria-hidden` for the same reason it always
   was: the colour is not information a screen reader can use, and the label
   beside it says the same thing in words. That is also the part the bare dot got
   wrong. On a board column header and a run's status line it was rendered with
   NO label at all, so a sighted reader saw a state and a screen reader saw
   nothing. Here `label` is required, so a dot can never again be the only thing
   carrying the meaning.

   THE TINT TABLE IS SHORTER THAN THE TONE LIST, and that is measured rather than
   sloppy. `css/tokens/color.src.css` defines five state colours
   (`running`, `attention`, `unassigned`, `lease`, `offline`), five `-tint`
   variants and only FOUR `-ink` variants: there is no `--color-state-offline-ink`,
   no `state-stale-*` and no `state-idle-*` at all. So three tones —
   `stale`, `offline`, `idle` — have no tint/ink pair to be tinted with and read
   on the quiet surface instead, while `stale` and `offline` keep the hollow ring
   that distinguished them on the dot. Inventing the missing tokens here would
   put a colour decision in a template.

   `wait` MAPS ONTO THE `unassigned` TOKEN PAIR. That is not a typo: the deleted
   pill mapped it the same way, because the red is the colour of "nobody is
   acting on this" and `wait` is the name the tone vocabulary gives it.

   `critical` MAPS ONTO THE SAME PAIR, and shares it rather than asking for a
   sixth state colour, because the token layer defines no critical hue and a
   template is the wrong place to choose one. Two tones over one pair is not a
   duplicate: `wait` says nobody is acting on this and `critical` says this one is
   first, and the second is what the absorbed `priority` variant rendered P1 with.
   Until then P1 borrowed `wait` — the right red under the wrong name.

   `lease` IS THE FIFTH PAIR AND THE ONE NOTHING REACHED. The indigo
   `--color-state-lease-tint`/`-ink` existed in the token layer while the badge
   that used it was folded in as `tone: neutral` + `icon: lock`, which lost the
   tint, the mono face and the outline in one step. `mono` and `mine` below are
   the other two thirds of that repair. #}
{% set tone_key = tone|default('neutral') %}
{% set quiet = 'bg-surface-3 text-text-light' %}
{% set tint_map = {
  'running': 'bg-state-running-tint text-state-running-ink',
  'attention': 'bg-state-attention-tint text-state-attention-ink',
  'wait': 'bg-state-unassigned-tint text-state-unassigned-ink',
  'critical': 'bg-state-unassigned-tint text-state-unassigned-ink',
  'lease': 'bg-state-lease-tint text-state-lease-ink'
} %}
{% set dot_map = {
  'running': 'bg-state-running',
  'attention': 'bg-state-attention',
  'wait': 'bg-state-unassigned',
  'critical': 'bg-state-unassigned',
  'lease': 'bg-state-lease',
  'stale': 'bg-transparent border-2 border-state-attention',
  'offline': 'bg-transparent border-2 border-state-offline'
} %}
{#- THE OUTLINE OF `mine`, IN THE TONE'S OWN COLOUR, at the width and the opacity
    the deleted claim badge drew it: one hairline at 45% of the state hue, which
    reads as a ring on the tint without becoming a second border. It is mapped
    per tone rather than hard-wired to indigo so that a `mine` badge on any tinted
    tone outlines in its own colour instead of in somebody else's. #}
{% set mine_map = {
  'running': 'outline outline-1 outline-state-running/45',
  'attention': 'outline outline-1 outline-state-attention/45',
  'wait': 'outline outline-1 outline-state-unassigned/45',
  'critical': 'outline outline-1 outline-state-unassigned/45',
  'lease': 'outline outline-1 outline-state-lease/45'
} %}
{% set badge_classes = [
  'inline-flex', 'max-w-full', 'min-w-0', 'overflow-x-clip', '*:min-w-0', 'items-center', 'gap-1', 'h-5', 'px-2',
  'rounded-sm', 'text-badge', 'whitespace-nowrap',
  tint_map[tone_key]|default(quiet),
] %}
{% if mono %}{% set badge_classes = badge_classes|merge(['font-mono']) %}{% endif %}
{% if mine and mine_map[tone_key] is defined %}{% set badge_classes = badge_classes|merge([mine_map[tone_key]]) %}{% endif %}
{#- `role="listitem"` is not derived and not guessed: a badge cannot know whether
    it is a direct child of a `role="list"` container, and a `listitem` outside a
    list is as wrong as a list without items. So the caller that sets
    `bb_theme:zone`'s `list: true` says so here too. It disappears the moment a
    container owns its own items. #}
<span{{ (attributes ?: create_attribute()).addClass(badge_classes) }}{% if list_item %} role="listitem"{% endif %}>
  {%- if dot %}<span class="inline-block shrink-0 w-2 h-2 rounded-full {{ dot_map[tone_key]|default('bg-state-offline') }}" aria-hidden="true"></span>{% endif %}
  {%- if icon %}{{ include('bb_theme:icon', { name: icon, size: 'xs' }, with_context = false) }}{% endif %}
  <span data-badge-label>{{ label }}</span>
  {%- if suffix %}<span class="text-badge font-normal tabular-nums text-text-faint">{{ suffix }}</span>{% endif %}
</span>
